Pemphigus Foliaceus (Mild Superficial)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Crusted patches on face/scalp/chest, often misdiagnosed as seborrheic dermatitis, mild burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-035
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe facial and scalp burning with rapidly spreading crusted lesions, requiring exclusion of a severe secondary bacterial or viral skin infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden, painful, and spreading crusted rash on the face. Immediate clinical review was mandatory to rule out acute eczema herpeticum, severe impetigo, or early Stevens-Johnson Syndrome prior to the next in-hours period to prevent rapid systemic deterioration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My face and scalp are covered in these burning, weeping crusts that seem to be multiplying by the hour. I'm worried it's a terrible infection spreading to my eyes.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports superficial scaling without intact blisters, present mildly for weeks and often misdiagnosed as seborrheic dermatitis. Today, the lesions on the face, scalp, and upper chest became acutely inflamed, displaying mild burning and weeping clear fluid. The rapid change in the skin's appearance caused the patient severe distress and fear of a disfiguring "flesh-eating" infection. Denies fevers, lip/mouth swelling, or eye involvement.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High aesthetic distress and anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, visibly distressed regarding facial appearance.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals multiple superficial, crusted, and scaling erythematous patches on the face, scalp, and upper chest ("seborrheic" distribution). The crusts are yellowish but not the thick "honey-colored" crusts of impetigo. No intact tense or flaccid bullae are visible. Nikolsky sign is positive (light rubbing of normal-appearing skin near a lesion induces superficial peeling). Oropharyngeal and conjunctival mucosa are completely clear. No regional lymphadenopathy or fever.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Utilizing over-the-counter anti-dandruff shampoos which have exacerbated the burning.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pemphigus Foliaceus Flare (Mild Superficial) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Eczema Herpeticum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of "punched-out" erosions, no systemic toxicity or fever, and lack of characteristic grouped vesicles.

Severe Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Superficial peeling nature of the rash rather than thick, purulent honey-colored crusts, and positive Nikolsky sign indicating a primary blistering process.

Stevens-Johnson Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of mucosal involvement (lips, eyes, mouth) and absence of a severe drug trigger or systemic illness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a rapid-onset, burning, and crusted facial eruption, a high-risk presentation demanding immediate exclusion of severe infectious processes like eczema herpeticum or impetigo, which require urgent antiviral/antibiotic therapy. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination. Bedside evaluation demonstrated a superficial, non-purulent peeling process with a positive Nikolsky sign, but strictly spared the mucosa, confirming an autoimmune superficial blistering disorder (Pemphigus Foliaceus) rather than a severe infection. This rapid assessment was critical to immediately cease aggravating topical treatments, provide reassurance, and initiate appropriate topical steroids, preventing an unnecessary emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the patient to immediately stop all harsh anti-dandruff shampoos and exfoliating cleansers. Prescribed a mild/moderate topical corticosteroid cream (e.g., hydrocortisone 1% or betamethasone valerate 0.02%) for the face and chest.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a very thin layer of plain, white petroleum jelly over the crusted areas immediately after gently washing with cool water. This will artificially create a protective skin barrier, instantly stopping the burning sensation and preventing the crusts from cracking and bleeding when you move your face.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 3-5 days to coordinate formal dermatology referral and potential skin biopsy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop any painful sores or blisters inside your mouth, if your eyes become red, sticky, and painful, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal dermatological biopsy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This condition causes the very top layer of the skin to lose its "glue," resulting in superficial peeling and crusting. It is not an infection and does not cause deep scarring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the weeping areas become thick with yellow-green pus, become extremely hot, or if the redness spreads rapidly across your entire body.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is mistakenly attacking the surface layer of your skin. Harsh soaps and rubbing are making the peeling much worse. The steroid cream will calm the immune cells locally.
Actionable Steps: Do not scrub or pick at the crusts. Use only lukewarm water to wash your face. You must see your GP soon to arrange a specialist review for long-term control.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Pemphigus Foliaceus (superficial immune peeling). No severe infection found. Mucosa is safe. Stop harsh soaps. Steroid cream prescribed. See GP in 3 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Stop using anti-dandruff shampoos or harsh soaps immediately. Apply petroleum jelly. Go to ED if you get mouth sores, eye pain, or a high fever.